    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_5e53234983b5124f3e4223b38a7a8c09a116d74eb9e536607781874bf45e4f3c",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Source-grounded mathematics: for an exact polynomial with the stated simple-root condition, a Sturm chain turns endpoint sign-variation differences into the number of real roots on [a,b]. The reported algorithm applies this to successive Jensen polynomials and uses Newton continuation only to approximate a complex root as a check. Conceptual interpretation: the computation is reframed from locating every root to deciding whether all roots are real. A root-count deficit supports the desired conclusion only when the interval contains every real root and the numerically evaluated Sturm signs are validated; floating-point sign computation is a separate obligation from the exact Sturm theorem. Uncertainty: interval and precision details continue in later units, and minor OCR substitutions affect polynomial notation.",
