    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_2b8e34f114cecea2c49b8b41d6e6de16f07c6d1ae6fa713b8827e77cc1f757bb",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "For this obstruction-search algorithm, the finite computations nearer zero combine two worsening features: the reported smallest Jensen degree with a nonreal witness rises from 16 at λ=-50 to 406 at λ=-5, while the displayed conjugate pair approaches the real axis. The method responds by replacing full root enumeration with a Sturm decision about whether all roots are real and by increasing working precision when sign sequences and counts become unstable, with interval enlargement addressing a separate possible cause of an apparent deficit. The table, algorithm choice, and diagnostics together explain the reported computational bottleneck without asserting a general complexity law.",
